MANDEL. Programs written by Andrew G. Moore


These programs give a graphical representation of the standard Mandelbrot
set showing the Mandelbrot bay and bayletts. The source for each program
is given and may be changed by others.


The Programs:

	MANDEL3.OPO	First written as a basic exercise. The two are
	MANDEL3a.OPO	machine dependent.

	MANDEL.IMG	Written because the first two run like a dog. There
			is a slight improvement on speed. Floating point
			arithmatic done without the 8087 emulator. Runs on
			either the Series 3 or Series 3a (dual mode).

	MANDELFP.IMG	Written for a further increase in speed. Again this
 			is written in dual mode and will run on either the
			Series 3 or Series 3a (using the 3a's larger screen
			size). This version uses the 8087 emulator, so this
			has to be available to the program.

	MANDELG.IMG	The same as MANDEL.IMG but this version uses the grey
			plane to show more colours. Because of this, it will
			only run on the Series 3a.


To install.

Install MANDEL3.OPO and MANDEL3a.OPO by copying these to a \OPO\ directory
and run by selecting them from the "Run OPL" list.

Install MANDEL.IMG and MANDELG.IMG by copying these filese to a \IMG\
directory and run by selecting them from the "Run IMG" list.

Install MANDEL.IMG and SYS$8087.LDD by copying these files to a \IMG\
directory and run by selecting MANDELFP from the "Run IMG" list.
Remember if SYS$8087.LDD is not copied the program will stop with a Panic 80.


When run the program will prompt you for X centre, Y centre, zoom and
infinity value. The X and Y centre positions the area you wish to see,
0,0 being the centre, while zoom indicates the portion. Infinity gives
the program a cut off point, a lower infinity will give a coarser image
but will run faster.

Try 0 for X centre, 0 for Y centre, 4 for zoom and 40 for infinity to get
a good overview of the entire set.

Please be patient, these programs can take some time to complete, though
they will run in the background.


